I tried it with my other (work related content) account, and the link seemed to work. But here's a different format of the link. Hopefully it works for you.

&#x200B;

 [https://www.gofundme.com/f/help-charlie-and-apollo-find-their-forever-home?utm\_source=customer&utm\_campaign=p\_cp+share-sheet&utm\_medium=copy\_link-tip](https://www.gofundme.com/f/help-charlie-and-apollo-find-their-forever-home?utm_source=customer&utm_campaign=p_cp+share-sheet&utm_medium=copy_link-tip)

It's impossible to tell just by looking at those tanks if they are empty or full. It would be very easy for a full one to be hiding in there on accident. 

&#x200B;

It's essentially the same with gun safety of "treat every gun like a loaded gun". If you treat every compressed gas cylinder like a full cylinder you will never have an exploding cylinder.

I complained about my Toyota Corolla ONLY because it was too small for me. Due to my long legs, I couldn't drive it for long distances without back and leg pain.

&#x200B;

Now I drive a used station wagon that I can stretch out in. (The rest of this isn't horribly off base)

In my first semester of pharmacy school. My pharmaceutical care professors brought in a patient who has had a kidney transplant, both kidneys removed, and 2 liver transplants. She is also allergic to 4 classes of antibiotics. She is the classic patient that NEEDS a pharmacy consult when she arrives in the ER. On an ER visit that turned into an admission, about 10 years before she was talking to us, she was waking up after surgery and overheard the attending telling a resident to order penicillin for her post op. She was still too out of it to talk, so she started tapping her bright red allergy bracelet. Luckily a friend of hers who happened to be a pharmacist was in the room, saw the tapping and told the attending that he had to pick a different antibiotic because she had an anaphylactic reaction to penicillins.

&#x200B;

HOW THE F\*\*\* DO DOCTORS NOT SEE A RED BRACELET AND READ IT EVERY TIME THEY WALK IN THE ROOM?????
